{
	title: 'Every scrollback clear passes a reason a plugin can log',
	spec: 'Invariant: the terminal client clears screen and scrollback (CSI 2J H 3J, the canonical repaint in src/client/render.ts) only by calling render.clearScrollback(reason), an exported function on the render module object, so a plugin can observe every clear with plugin.before(render, \'clearScrollback\', (reason) => ...). The reason is a plain object naming the cause and its evidence: a forced repaint and its trigger (Ctrl-L or /redraw, resize, resume, tab switch, the first paint with two or more tabs, the top out of reach after the terminal shrank); a frame shrink (old and new row counts, the first changed row, and the blocks whose rows shrank, with block ids such as #t239 and what changed, e.g. tool output collapsed, card closed); or a change to a row already in scrollback (the row, the writable top, the block id and what changed). It also holds one plain-English sentence explaining the snap. Core Hal records nothing: logging is opt-in through a local plugin only. A generic example plugin, a task artifact, appends each reason with the local time and the shown session id as one line to an owner-only (0600) file in the client home state directory, rejects symlinks and keeps the newest 500, so a user can ask a session in the Hal home "why did that snap" and the model answers from that file. Reasons hold no transcript text, tool arguments or output, typed keys, URLs, credentials, hostnames or paths. Behavioral tests: each clear path calls clearScrollback with its cause; a shrink names the shrinking block.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['4k'],
	foldInto: ['4k', 'cc']
}
